            <title>[LU-21049] TCU: avoid orphan MDTXXXX trash object when MDT setup Trash Can</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21049</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;There are these log messages on MDT0002 on mount time. Those only occur on the failed session:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="codeContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre class="code-java"&gt;

lustre-MDT0000-osp-MDT0002: next log does not exist!
top_trans_stop(): lustre-MDT0000-osp-MDT0002: write updates failed: rc = -5
mdt_trash_setup_thread(): lustre-MDT0002: Trash dir setup failed: rc = -5
&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And this is what Claude said:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;At mount, mdt_trash_setup_thread() creates .lustre/.ltrash/MDT0002. The directory object lives on MDT0002 but its name entry is on MDT0000, so the create spans two MDTs.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The setup thread ran before the update log between MDT0002 and MDT0000 was ready, so the first attempt failed with -EIO. It still left a half-made object, &lt;span class="error"&gt;&amp;#91;0x280000400:0x2:0x0&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The thread retried and created a second object, &lt;span class="error"&gt;&amp;#91;0x280000400:0x5:0x0&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;, which succeeded without logging anything more.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;test_118z's cleanup runs a namespace LFSCK and fails on any repair. The MDT0002 debug log shows LFSCK finding both objects claiming the name MDT0002 under the trash dir &lt;span class="error"&gt;&amp;#91;0x200000002:0x4:0x0&amp;#93;&lt;/span&gt;. It moved 0x2 to lost+found, which gives multiple_referenced_repaired: 1 and the "Expect no namespace repair, but got 1" error.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;


&lt;p&gt;Verdict: the race is in existing trash-setup code that the patch doesn't touch (it doesn't change mdt_handler.c). 118z only reports it because its cleanup runs the first LFSCK after mount. Repeating the test in a custom session won't reproduce it, because the race happens once per mount, not once per test run.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
